<hr>
<h2>Investigating Suspicious LNK Files</h2>
<p>In this scenario, we will explore how some suspicious artifacts or IOCs =
can be extracted from LNK files, as these are heavily abused by threat acto=
rs for initial access.</p>
<p>For the demonstration, we have a shortcut file (LNK file) saved in the D=
emo directory of the evidence at <code>C:\Tools\DFIR-Data\evidence\005.lnk\=
Demo</code>.</p>
<p><img src=3D"https://academy.hackthebox.com/storage/modules/248/lnk-susp-=
1.png" alt=3D"File Explorer window showing the &quot;Demo&quot; folder cont=
aining a PDF file named &quot;Invoice-details.pdf&quot;."></p>
<p>This shortcut appears to be for a PDF file. However, if we run this file=
, it executes an HTA application. In the target of the LNK file, it contain=
s the path to <code>mshta.exe</code> with a URL as an argument.</p>
<p><img src=3D"https://academy.hackthebox.com/storage/modules/248/lnk-mshta=
-01.png" alt=3D"Properties window for &quot;Invoice-details.pdf&quot; showi=
ng target type as Application, target location as System32, and target path=
 as C:\Windows\System32\mshta.exe https://rb.gy/...."></p>
<p>We can run <code>LECmd.exe</code> to get more details.</p>
<div class=3D"window_container"><div class=3D"window_content">
                      <div class=3D"window_top">
                          <span class=3D"window_dot bg-danger"></span>
                          <span class=3D"window_dot bg-warning"></span>
                          <span class=3D"window_dot bg-success"></span>
                          <span class=3D"window_title">LNK Files</span>
                      </div>
                  <pre class=3D" language-cmd-session" style=3D"background:=
 black; line-height: 0px;"><code class=3D" language-cmd-session" style=3D"c=
olor: white;">C:&gt; C:\Tools\DFIR-Data\Tools\EZ-Tools\net6\LECmd.exe -f "C=
:\Tools\DFIR-Data\evidence\005.lnk\Demo\Invoice-details.pdf.lnk"
</code></pre></div></div>
<p>Since the target application is HTA, the argument is most likely an <cod=
e>.HTA</code> file.</p>
<p><img src=3D"https://academy.hackthebox.com/storage/modules/248/lnk-mshta=
1-1.png" alt=3D"Text showing link information with flags: VolumeIdAndLocalB=
asePath. Volume details include drive type as fixed storage media, serial n=
umber B444F7C7, label OS, and local path C:\Windows\System32\mshta.exe. Abs=
olute path is My Computer\C:\Windows\System32\mshta.exe."></p>
<p>In the output from <code>LECMD.exe</code>, we can see that the arguments=
 contain a URL, which is most probably a HTA file.</p>
<p><img src=3D"https://academy.hackthebox.com/storage/modules/248/lnk-mshta=
-002.png" alt=3D"Text showing LECmd processing Invoice-details.pdf.lnk. Inc=
ludes source and target dates, file size, attributes, and a suspicious URL =
in arguments: https://rb.gy/6ansbg."></p>
<p>This also contains information about the machine where the LNK file was =
created and the time it was created.</p>
<p><img src=3D"https://academy.hackthebox.com/storage/modules/248/lnk_machi=
ne1.png" alt=3D"Text showing known folder data block with GUID for System32=
. Tracker database block includes machine ID htbvm01, MAC address, and crea=
tion date. Volume and file droid details are also listed."></p>
<hr>
<h3>Real world malware samples using LNK files</h3>
<p>Here's one <a href=3D"https://blog.eclecticiq.com/operation-flightnight-=
indian-government-entities-and-energy-sector-targeted-by-cyber-espionage-ca=
mpaign" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener nofollow">example</a> where we ca=
n see two different malware variants using the same LNK file created on the=
 same machine.</p>
<p>In the first malware variant, the threat actor is using a modified versi=
on of <a href=3D"https://github.com/moonD4rk/HackBrowserData" target=3D"_bl=
ank" rel=3D"noopener nofollow">HackBrowserData</a>, an open-source post-exp=
loitation tool. This variant is bundled as an ISO file that contains a deco=
y PDF document, malware in executable form, and a shortcut file (LNK file) =
intended to trick recipients into activating the malware.</p>
<p><img src=3D"https://academy.hackthebox.com/storage/modules/248/lnk_machi=
ne2.png" alt=3D"File Explorer showing a PDF shortcut &quot;invitation-lette=
r&quot; and a folder. Command Prompt displays tracker database block with m=
achine ID desktop-e7n7e7f, MAC address, and creation date 2023-11-30 03:35:=
36. Arguments include /c start /B .\t\scholar.exe &amp; .\t\invitation.pdf.=
"></p>
<p>The second screenshot shown below is from a different variant, which use=
s GoStealer (a Golang-based credential stealer) instead of HackBrowserData.=
 The rest of the delivery mechanism is the same. It also contains an ISO fi=
le with a decoy PDF document, an executable, and a shortcut file (LNK file)=
.</p>
<p><img src=3D"https://academy.hackthebox.com/storage/modules/248/lnk_machi=
ne3.png" alt=3D"Text showing working directory and arguments: /c start /B .=
temp.tmp.exe &amp; .temp\sample.pdf. Icon location is C:\Program Files (x86=
)\Microsoft\Edge\Application\msedge.exe. Volume information includes drive =
type, serial number, and local path. Tracker database block shows machine I=
D desktop-e7n7e7f, MAC address, and creation date 2023-11-30 03:35:36."></p=
>
<p>The interesting finding from the analysis of LNK files is that the machi=
ne ID and creation time are the same in both variants, which provides stron=
g evidence that both campaigns are likely the work of the same threat actor=
.</p>
<hr>
<p>In <a href=3D"https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/bumblebee-m=
alware-adds-post-exploitation-tool-for-stealthy-infections/" target=3D"_bla=
nk" rel=3D"noopener nofollow">this blog</a> from Bleeping Computer, it is m=
entioned that adversaries use LNK files for initial access or execution of =
payloads. That's also why LNK metadata is essential from a forensics perspe=
ctive.</p>
<ul>
<li>"Bumblebee reached victims via emails carrying password-protected zippe=
d ISO files that contained an LNK file (for executing the payload) and a DL=
L file (the payload)."</li>
<li>"In the recent attack, Bumblebee replaced the ISO with a VHD (Virtual H=
ard Disk) file, which, again, contains an LNK shortcut file."</li>
<li>"The Emotet malware distributors launched a new email campaign that inc=
luded password-protected ZIP file attachments containing Windows LNK (short=
cut) files masquerading as Word documents."</li>
</ul>
